\title{Comparative Benchmark of State-of-Charge Observers and Physics-Informed Neural Networks Across Battery Aging and Thermal Regimes}

\keywords{State-of-charge, Lithium-ion battery, Battery degradation, Dual Extended Kalman filter, Physics-informed neural network, Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy, Embedded BMS, ASME JEECS}

\begin{abstract}
While battery State-of-Charge (SOC) estimation has been extensively studied, existing comparative benchmarks predominantly evaluate fresh cells at room temperature, obscuring algorithmic vulnerabilities under operational aging and thermal extremes. This study establishes a rigorous multi-attribute benchmark evaluating representative nonlinear observers, deep recurrent networks, and physics-informed models (EKF, AEKF, DEKF, SMO, BiLSTM-Attn, and PINN-SOC) across standardized driving cycles. Algorithms are systematically benchmarked across progressive degradation ($100\% \rightarrow 80\%$ SOH), wide thermal regimes ($0^\circ\text{C}$ to $45^\circ\text{C}$), flat-plateau $\text{LiFePO}_4$ chemistry, and series 8S pack inconsistency. Findings reveal that while uncompensated observers suffer catastrophic 45-fold accuracy decay as internal impedance grows, parameter-tracking DEKF confines lifespan RMSE to $0.585\%$. Furthermore, we propose a lightweight hybrid Physics-Informed Dual Kalman Filter (PIDKF) that achieves $0.414\%$ RMSE on degraded NMC and $0.149\%$ on degraded LFP cells. Finally, cycle-accurate profiling on ARM Cortex-M microcontrollers maps the empirical Pareto efficiency frontier, providing actionable selection guidelines for industrial battery management systems.
\end{abstract}

