%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Introduction}

The global transition toward carbon-neutral transportation and renewable grid energy storage has accelerated the deployment of high-energy-density lithium-ion battery (LIB) packs~\cite{Tarascon2001,Lu2013review,Mukherjee2024}. In automotive battery management systems (BMS), the State-of-Charge (SOC) represents the ratio of currently available capacity to maximum usable capacity. SOC serves as the fundamental state metric required for accurate driving range estimation, optimal regenerative braking control, prevention of over-charge and over-discharge, and dynamic power capability (State-of-Power, SOP) calculation~\cite{Plett2004part1,Xiong2018joint,Farmann2016peukert}. 

Unlike terminal voltage or operating temperature, the SOC cannot be directly measured via physical sensors during vehicle operation due to complex internal solid-state diffusion, interfacial charge-transfer kinetics, and structural phase transitions occurring within the porous insertion electrodes~\cite{Doyle1993,Torchio2016pybam}. Consequently, online SOC estimation must rely on mathematical observers that interpret terminal current, voltage, and surface temperature signals~\cite{Plett2004part2,Hu2012ecm,Ren2019aging}.

\subsection{Taxonomy of Algorithmic Estimation Paradigms}
Over the past two decades, battery state estimation methodologies in electrochemical literature have evolved into five primary algorithmic paradigms~\cite{Xiong2018joint,Hannan2021review}:

1. \emph{Direct Empirical Integration}: Coulomb counting (current integration) constitutes the primary baseline in the automotive industry due to minimal computational complexity $\mathcal{O}(1)$~\cite{Plett2004part1}. However, it suffers from open-loop error accumulation caused by current sensor bias drift, analog-to-digital converter (ADC) quantization, and unmodeled parasitic reactions. Open-Circuit Voltage (OCV) lookup methods achieve static accuracy below $1\%$ but require extensive cell relaxation periods ($30\text{--}120\text{ minutes}$) to extinguish diffusion overpotentials, rendering them inapplicable during active vehicle driving~\cite{Roscher2011hysteresis,Verbrugge2004hysteresis}.

2. \emph{Model-Based Nonlinear Observers}: To provide closed-loop error correction, equivalent circuit models (ECM)—most notably the first-order Thevenin and second-order Dual Polarization (2-RC) models---are coupled with recursive filtering theory~\cite{He2011ecm,Hu2012ecm}. The Extended Kalman Filter (EKF) linearizes nonlinear OCV relationships via first-order Taylor expansion~\cite{Plett2004part3,Santhanagopalan2006jes}. To handle unknown or time-varying sensor noise statistics, Adaptive EKF (AEKF) formulations dynamically estimate process and measurement noise covariances via Sage-Husa recursions~\cite{Han2019aekf,Wang2021tcn}. Sigma-point filters, such as the Unscented Kalman Filter (UKF) and Cubature Kalman Filter (CKF), propagate deterministically chosen sigma points to achieve third-order Taylor accuracy without explicit Jacobian evaluation~\cite{Plett2006ukf,He2013ukf,Peng2021ackf}. 

To counteract parametric drift caused by solid-electrolyte interphase (SEI) growth, Dual Extended Kalman Filters (DEKF) run two concurrent filters: one estimating fast electromagnetic/polarization states and the second tracking slow electrochemical parameters ($R_0, Q_{\text{act}}$)~\cite{Charkhgard2010dekf,Yang2024degradation}. In parallel, Sliding Mode Observers (SMO) enforce error trajectories onto a discontinuous switching surface, offering robust performance against bounded parameter uncertainties~\cite{Kim2011,Chen2016smo,Feng2019smo}.

3. \emph{Physics-Based Electrochemical Observers}: Originating from the Doyle-Fuller-Newman (P2D) porous electrode theory, electrochemical models describe solid/liquid phase lithium concentrations, potential distributions, and Butler-Volmer reaction kinetics~\cite{Doyle1993,Torchio2016pybam,Northrop2014p2d}. Simplified representations, such as the Single Particle Model (SPM) and SPM with electrolyte dynamics (SPMe), enable partial differential equation (PDE) observer design~\cite{DiDomenico2010spm,Moura2017pde,Marquis2019spme}. Despite their physical fidelity, real-time implementation on automotive microcontrollers remains constrained by heavy numerical PDE discretization burdens.

4. \emph{Pure Data-Driven Machine Learning}: Advances in deep learning have prompted widespread exploration of neural network architectures for battery state prognosis~\cite{Hansen2005svr,Chemali2018dnn,Liu2020gpr}. Recurrent architectures, including Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), Bidirectional LSTM (BiLSTM), Gated Recurrent Units (GRU), and Temporal Convolutional Networks (TCN), capture long-term temporal dependencies in dynamic loading profiles~\cite{Song2019bilstm,Jiao2020attn,Wang2021tcn}. Recently, multi-head self-attention mechanisms and Transformers have been applied to highlight critical electrochemical transition states~\cite{Vaswani2017attention,Shen2023transformer}. Nevertheless, pure data-driven models behave as black boxes, lacking physical guarantees, and exhibit severe performance collapse when deployed outside their narrow training distribution.

5. \emph{Physics-Informed Machine Learning}: To bridge empirical observations and electrochemical principles, Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs) embed governing differential-algebraic conservation equations (Faradaic species conservation, Kirchhoff's voltage laws, and thermodynamic monotonicity constraints) directly into the neural backpropagation objective~\cite{Raissi2019pinn,Brancato2026,Li2023nature}. By evaluating physics residual gradients via automatic differentiation across dense unsupervised collocation domains, PINNs constrain the neural parameter manifold to physically admissible trajectories, mitigating out-of-distribution divergence without the parameter sensitivity of classical observers.

\subsection{Critical Review of Existing Benchmark Studies and Knowledge Gaps}
Despite thousands of published papers on SOC estimation, existing comparative literature exhibits five fundamental deficiencies:

\emph{Gap 1: Pristine Cell Bias and Neglect of Progressive Degradation}. The vast majority of published studies validate proposed algorithms solely on fresh cells ($\text{SOH} = 100\%$) at beginning-of-life (BOL)~\cite{Hu2012ecm,Chemali2018dnn,Le2026charging}. In electric vehicle applications, batteries experience progressive capacity fade and impedance growth down to $80\%$ SOH (end-of-life, EOL). The sensitivity of uncompensated observers to progressive SEI growth has not been systematically quantified.

\emph{Gap 2: Decoupling of Thermal Kinetics from Observer Dynamics}. Battery polarization and charge-transfer resistance exhibit exponential Arrhenius temperature dependence~\cite{He2011ecm,Ren2019aging}. Prior benchmarks predominantly conduct evaluations at room temperature ($25^\circ\text{C}$), leaving the comparative resilience of observers under sub-zero ($0^\circ\text{C}$) and elevated ($45^\circ\text{C}$) conditions poorly understood.

\emph{Gap 3: Observability Degradation on Flat-Plateau Chemistries}. While nickel-manganese-cobalt (NMC) chemistries possess relatively steep OCV-SOC slopes across their operating window, lithium iron phosphate ($\text{LiFePO}_4$, LFP) cells exhibit an extremely flat voltage plateau across $20\%\text{--}80\%$ SOC ($d\text{OCV}/d\text{SOC} \approx 0$) accompanied by pronounced dynamic hysteresis~\cite{Roscher2011hysteresis,Verbrugge2004hysteresis,Theiler2024hysteresis}. Tian et al.~\cite{Tian2025} recently demonstrated that mechanical expansion force fusion can mitigate this issue, but the capability of purely electrical observers to track LFP flat plateaus without costly force sensors remains unbenchmarked.

\emph{Gap 4: Multi-Source Sensor Corruption Vulnerability}. In operational EV battery packs, Hall-effect current transducers suffer from zero-point thermal bias drift, and voltage sensing lines are corrupted by high-frequency electromagnetic interference (EMI) induced by inverter pulse-width modulation (PWM) switching~\cite{Hashemi2020,Zheng2018inconsistency}. Benchmark literature rarely evaluates observers under combined sensor drift and EMI noise stress.

\emph{Gap 5: Disconnect Between Algorithmic Complexity and Embedded Hardware Feasibility}. Complex machine learning and high-order observer architectures are typically evaluated in desktop Python/MATLAB environments equipped with multi-core CPUs or GPUs~\cite{Song2019bilstm,Bhattacharya2024gpr}. Quantitative profiling on commercial automotive microcontrollers (e.g., ARM Cortex-M4/M7) regarding floating-point execution time, static RAM, and Flash memory consumption is virtually absent. Furthermore, the translation of cell-level observers to series-connected packs subject to cell-to-cell parameter inconsistency is seldom examined~\cite{Yang2024degradation,Zheng2018inconsistency}.

\subsection{Contributions of This Work}
To resolve these open challenges, this paper establishes an exhaustive benchmark evaluating 6 foundational and modern state estimation methodologies across 10 distinct operational stress phases. Key contributions include:

1. \emph{Comprehensive Multi-Attribute Evaluation Across 10 Operational Phases}: We benchmark EKF, AEKF, DEKF, SMO, BiLSTM-Attention, and PINN-SOC across standardized driving schedules (UDDS, US06, DST, FUDS), 5 progressive aging stages ($100\%$ to $80\%$ SOH), 3 ambient temperatures ($0^\circ\text{C}, 25^\circ\text{C}, 45^\circ\text{C}$), large initial state uncertainty ($\Delta\text{SOC}_0 = -40\%$), LFP flat plateaus, sensor bias/EMI corruptions, and 8S series pack inconsistency.

2. \emph{Mathematical Formulation of the Aging Sensitivity Factor (ASF)}: We define and quantify the Aging Sensitivity Factor, demonstrating an alarming 45-fold accuracy degradation in standard EKF as SOH fades to 80\%, while parameter-tracking DEKF bounds maximum error growth.

3. \emph{Electrochemical Impedance Spectroscopy (EIS) Frequency-Domain Bridge}: We map time-domain observer tracking drift directly to physical Nyquist impedance spectra ($100\text{ kHz}$ to $1\text{ mHz}$), revealing how charge-transfer and SEI resistance expansion govern observer divergent tendencies.

4. \emph{Novel Hybrid Architecture: Physics-Informed Dual Kalman Filter (PIDKF)}: We propose a computationally lightweight hybrid architecture that augments DEKF parameter co-estimation with physics-informed parameter priors, dynamic hysteresis tracking, and neural polarization compensation, suppressing unmodeled polarization residuals and reducing RMSE to $0.414\%$ on degraded NMC and $0.149\%$ on degraded LFP cells ($85\%$ SOH).

5. \emph{Embedded Profiling on ARM Cortex-M Architectures}: We quantify cycle-accurate execution complexity on ARM Cortex-M4 and Cortex-M7 architectures via CMSIS-DSP, mapping the empirical Pareto efficiency frontier and providing actionable selection criteria for industrial BMS deployment.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Electrochemical Battery Modeling and Degradation Dynamics}

\subsection{Second-Order Dual Polarization (2-RC) Model Formulation}
The electrical behavior of the lithium-ion cell is represented by a second-order Dual Polarization equivalent circuit model, widely acknowledged as the optimal balance between mathematical tractability and dynamic fidelity~\cite{Hu2012ecm,Xiong2014}. The circuit comprises an open-circuit voltage source $V_{\text{ocv}}(\text{SOC})$, an ohmic series resistance $R_0$, and two parallel resistance-capacitance ($R_i \parallel C_i$) networks representing fast charge-transfer interfacial kinetics and slow solid-state lithium diffusion processes, respectively:
\begin{align}
\frac{d\,\text{SOC}(t)}{dt} &= -\frac{\eta(T)}{3600\,Q_{\text{act}}}\,I(t), \label{eqn:soc_diff}\\
\frac{d U_1(t)}{dt} &= -\frac{U_1(t)}{R_1 C_1} + \frac{I(t)}{C_1}, \quad \frac{d U_2(t)}{dt} = -\frac{U_2(t)}{R_2 C_2} + \frac{I(t)}{C_2} \label{eqn:u12_diff}
\end{align}
where $I(t)$ denotes load current (defined positive for discharge and negative for charge), $\eta(T, I)$ represents Faradaic coulombic efficiency ($\eta = 1$ during discharge $I \ge 0$, and $\eta \in (0, 1]$ during charge $I < 0$ to capture parasitic side reactions), and $Q_{\text{act}}$ represents actual usable capacity in ampere-hours ($\text{A}\cdot\text{h}$). The terminal output voltage $V_t(t)$ satisfies Kirchhoff's voltage law:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:vt_diff}
V_t(t) = V_{\text{ocv}}(\text{SOC}(t)) - U_1(t) - U_2(t) - I(t) R_0.
\end{equation}
Under discharge ($I(t) > 0$), internal polarization overpotentials $U_i(t) \ge 0$ and bulk ohmic drop $I(t) R_0 > 0$ subtract from the thermodynamic potential, ensuring $V_t < V_{\text{ocv}}$. Net cell power output $P_{\text{cell}}(t) = V_t I = V_{\text{ocv}} I - (U_1 + U_2 + I R_0) I$ strictly satisfies the Second Law of Thermodynamics, where internal irreversible dissipation rate $\dot{S}_{\text{irr}} = (U_1 + U_2 + I R_0) I / T \ge 0$ represents non-negative entropy generation via Joule and kinetic polarization losses.

Discretizing the continuous state equations with sampling interval $\Delta t = 1.0\text{ s}$ via zero-order hold (ZOH) under the standard assumption that $I(t) = I_k$ remains constant over interval $t \in [t_k, t_{k+1})$ yields the discrete-time state-space representation. Evaluating the continuous convolution integral $\int_0^{\Delta t} \exp(-(\Delta t - s)/\tau_i) \frac{I_k}{C_i}\,ds = R_i [1 - \exp(-\Delta t/\tau_i)] I_k$ (noting $\tau_i = R_i C_i$) establishes the exact discrete-time equivalent:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:state_space_discrete}
\mathbf{x}_{k+1} = \mathbf{A}_k \mathbf{x}_k + \mathbf{B}_k I_k + \mathbf{w}_k,
\end{equation}
where the state vector is $\mathbf{x}_k = [\text{SOC}_k, U_{1,k}, U_{2,k}]^T$, the process noise vector is $\mathbf{w}_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \mathbf{Q}_k)$, and the system matrices are given by:
\begin{align}
\mathbf{A}_k &= \begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & \exp\left(-\frac{\Delta t}{\tau_1}\right) & 0 \\
0 & 0 & \exp\left(-\frac{\Delta t}{\tau_2}\right)
\end{bmatrix}, \label{eqn:Ak_mat} \\
\mathbf{B}_k &= \begin{bmatrix}
B_{1,k} \\ B_{2,k} \\ B_{3,k}
\end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix}
-\frac{\eta \Delta t}{3600\,Q_{\text{act}}} \\
R_1\left[1 - \exp\left(-\frac{\Delta t}{\tau_1}\right)\right] \\
R_2\left[1 - \exp\left(-\frac{\Delta t}{\tau_2}\right)\right]
\end{bmatrix}, \label{eqn:Bk_mat}
\end{align}
with polarization time constants $\tau_1 = R_1 C_1$ and $\tau_2 = R_2 C_2$ (calibrated at $25^\circ\text{C}$ to $\tau_1 = 24.0\text{ s}, \tau_2 = 112.5\text{ s}$ for NMC; $37.5\text{ s}, 144.0\text{ s}$ for LFP). The nonlinear observation equation is:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:measurement_eq}
y_k = V_k = V_{\text{ocv}}(\text{SOC}_k) - U_{1,k} - U_{2,k} - I_k R_0 + v_k,
\end{equation}
where $v_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0, R_k)$ represents measurement noise.

\subsection{Arrhenius Thermal-Kinetic Coupling}
Electrochemical reaction rates and ionic transport in liquid electrolyte obey Arrhenius kinetics~\cite{He2011ecm,Ren2019aging}. The temperature dependence of ohmic internal resistance $R_0(T)$ and polarization resistances $R_i(T)$ is modeled via:
\begin{align}
R_0(T) &= R_{0,\text{ref}} \exp\left[\frac{E_{a,0}}{R}\left(\frac{1}{T} - \frac{1}{T_{\text{ref}}}\right)\right] \label{eqn:arrh_r0}\\
R_i(T) &= R_{i,\text{ref}} \exp\left[\frac{E_{a,i}}{R}\left(\frac{1}{T} - \frac{1}{T_{\text{ref}}}\right)\right], \quad i \in \{1, 2\} \label{eqn:arrh_ri}
\end{align}
where $T_{\text{ref}} = 298.15\text{ K}$ ($25^\circ\text{C}$), $R = 8.314\text{ J}\cdot\text{mol}^{-1}\cdot\text{K}^{-1}$, and $E_{a,0}, E_{a,i}$ represent apparent activation energies for electrolyte conduction and interfacial charge transfer ($E_{a,0} \approx 20.5\text{ kJ}\cdot\text{mol}^{-1}$, $E_{a,1} \approx 28.4\text{ kJ}\cdot\text{mol}^{-1}$). At $0^\circ\text{C}$, electrolyte viscosity rises, resulting in an exact 2.13-fold increase in $R_0$ and 2.85-fold increase in $R_1$ via Eqs.~\eqref{eqn:arrh_r0}--\eqref{eqn:arrh_ri} (averaging $\sim 2.5\times$ total internal impedance surge).

\subsection{Degradation Physics: SEI Growth and Capacity Fade}
Battery aging involves two primary degradation modes: Loss of Lithium Inventory (LLI) driven by continuous growth of the Solid-Electrolyte Interphase (SEI) layer on the graphitic anode, and Loss of Active Material (LAM) driven by mechanical particle cracking and transition metal dissolution~\cite{Birkl2017diagnostics,Ren2019aging}:
\begin{align}
Q_{\text{act}}(\text{SOH}) &= Q_{\text{nom}} \times \left[0.80 + 0.20 \times \left(\frac{\text{SOH} - 0.80}{0.20}\right)\right] \label{eqn:aging_q}\\
R_0(\text{SOH}) &= R_{0,\text{nom}} \times \left[1.0 + 2.50 \times (1.0 - \text{SOH})\right] \label{eqn:aging_r0}
\end{align}
where nominal capacity is defined per chemistry ($Q_{\text{nom,NMC}} = 2.00\text{ A}\cdot\text{h}$ for Samsung INR18650-20R and $Q_{\text{nom,LFP}} = 2.30\text{ A}\cdot\text{h}$ for CALCE A123 26650). Across the degradation span ($\text{SOH} = 1.0 \rightarrow 0.80$), usable capacity scales as $Q_{\text{act}} = \text{SOH} \cdot Q_{\text{nom}}$, while bulk ohmic resistance expands by $50\%$ (from $35.0\text{ m}\Omega$ to $52.5\text{ m}\Omega$ for NMC).

\subsection{OCV-SOC Monotonicity and Dynamic Hysteresis}
To prevent artificial divergence in gradient-based observers (Runge's phenomenon), the $V_{\text{ocv}}(\text{SOC})$ function for NMC chemistry is strictly calibrated as a monotonically increasing 5th-order polynomial satisfying $d V_{\text{ocv}} / d\text{SOC} > 0$ for all $\text{SOC} \in [0, 1]$:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:ocv_poly}
V_{\text{ocv}}(z) = p_5 z^5 + p_4 z^4 + p_3 z^3 + p_2 z^2 + p_1 z + p_0,
\end{equation}
where $z \equiv \text{SOC}$, with calibrated polynomial coefficients: $p_5 = 9.204$, $p_4 = -28.841$, $p_3 = 33.376$, $p_2 = -17.419$, $p_1 = 4.674$, and $p_0 = 3.212$. Differentiating yields the exact quartic derivative:
$V_{\text{ocv}}'(z) = 46.020 z^4 - 115.364 z^3 + 100.128 z^2 - 34.838 z + 4.674$. All four roots of $V_{\text{ocv}}'(z) = 0$ are complex conjugate pairs ($z_{1,2} \approx 0.964 \pm 0.156 j$, $z_{3,4} \approx 0.289 \pm 0.151 j$), guaranteeing $V_{\text{ocv}}'(z) > 0$ strictly for all $z \in \mathbb{R}$. Over the physical domain $z \in [0, 1]$, $V_{\text{ocv}}'(z) \in [\alpha, V'_{\max}] = [0.481, 4.674]\text{ V}$, bounded away from zero.

Linearizing around an operating point $(\mathbf{x}_k, I_k)$, the discrete 3-step observability matrix $\mathcal{O}_k = [\mathbf{H}_k^T, (\mathbf{H}_k \mathbf{A})^T, (\mathbf{H}_k \mathbf{A}^2)^T]^T$ has a Vandermonde-structured determinant:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:det_obs}
\det(\mathcal{O}_k) = V_{\text{ocv}}'(z_k) (1 - a_1)(1 - a_2)(a_1 - a_2),
\end{equation}
Since $\tau_1 = 24.0\text{ s} \ne \tau_2 = 112.5\text{ s}$ ($a_1 \ne a_2$), $\Delta t > 0$, and $V_{\text{ocv}}'(z) \ge \alpha > 0$, we have $\det(\mathcal{O}_k) \ne 0$ and $\operatorname{rank}(\mathcal{O}_k) = 3$ everywhere on $z \in [0, 1]$, guaranteeing uniform local observability. Conversely, when $V_{\text{ocv}}'(z) \to 0$ (as in the LFP flat plateau where $V_{\text{ocv}}' \approx 0.08\text{ V}$), $\det(\mathcal{O}_k)$ shrinks by an order of magnitude and condition number $\kappa(\mathcal{O}_k)$ surges from $3.2 \times 10^4$ to $> 1.99 \times 10^5$, establishing the analytical root cause of flat-plateau observability degradation.

For $\text{LiFePO}_4$ (LFP) chemistry, open-circuit voltage exhibits a wide, flat biphasic transition plateau ($\text{LiFePO}_4 \leftrightarrow \text{FePO}_4$) where $d V_{\text{ocv}} / dz \approx 0.08\text{ V}$ between $20\%$ and $80\%$ SOC, accompanied by non-negligible dynamic hysteresis~\cite{Roscher2011hysteresis,Verbrugge2004hysteresis}:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:hysteresis_diff}
\frac{d V_h(t)}{dt} = -\beta \left| \frac{\eta I(t)}{3600\,Q_{\text{act}}} \right| V_h(t) + \beta \left| \frac{\eta I(t)}{3600\,Q_{\text{act}}} \right| M(z) \operatorname{sgn}(I(t)),
\end{equation}
where $V_h(t)$ is hysteresis voltage, $\beta$ is decay rate, and $M(z)$ is maximum hysteresis voltage ($M \approx 20\text{--}40\text{ mV}$). Because $\dot{z} = -\frac{\eta I}{3600 Q_{\text{act}}}$, evaluating Eq.~\eqref{eqn:hysteresis_diff} across interval $[t_k, t_{k+1})$ under zero-order hold (ZOH) current $I_k$ gives constant rate $\gamma_k = \beta \frac{|\Delta z_k|}{\Delta t}$, yielding the exact discrete-time analytical solution:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:hysteresis_discrete}
V_{h,k+1} = e^{-\beta |\Delta z_k|} V_{h,k} + \left(1 - e^{-\beta |\Delta z_k|}\right) M(z_k) \operatorname{sgn}(I_k),
\end{equation}
with $|\Delta z_k| = \frac{\eta |I_k| \Delta t}{3600 Q_{\text{act}}}$. Because $\lambda_k = e^{-\beta |\Delta z_k|} \in (0, 1]$, Eq.~\eqref{eqn:hysteresis_discrete} represents a strict convex combination between prior hysteresis $V_{h,k}$ and target envelope $M(z_k)\operatorname{sgn}(I_k)$. By contraction mapping, $|V_{h,k+1}| \le \max(|V_{h,k}|, M(z_k)) \le M_{\max}$, proving that the compact set $[-M_{\max}, M_{\max}]$ is positively invariant and unphysical divergence is fundamentally impossible.

For LFP chemistry, the state vector is augmented with dynamic hysteresis: $\mathbf{x}_k^{\text{LFP}} = [\text{SOC}_k, U_{1,k}, U_{2,k}, V_{h,k}]^T$. The discrete evolution follows $\mathbf{x}_{k+1}^{\text{LFP}} = \mathbf{A}_k^{\text{LFP}} \mathbf{x}_k^{\text{LFP}} + \mathbf{B}_k^{\text{LFP}} I_k + \mathbf{g}_k + \mathbf{w}_k$ with $\mathbf{A}_k^{\text{LFP}} = \operatorname{diag}(1, e^{-\Delta t/\tau_1}, e^{-\Delta t/\tau_2}, e^{-\beta|\Delta z_k|})$, $\mathbf{B}_k^{\text{LFP}} = [B_{1,k}, B_{2,k}, B_{3,k}, 0]^T$, and nonlinear excitation $\mathbf{g}_k = [0, 0, 0, (1 - e^{-\beta |\Delta z_k|}) M(z_k) \operatorname{sgn}(I_k)]^T$. The terminal voltage satisfies:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:vt_lfp}
V_{t,k} = V_{\text{ocv}}(\text{SOC}_k) - V_{h,k} - U_{1,k} - U_{2,k} - I_k R_0 + v_k,
\end{equation}
where the negative sign before $V_{h,k}$ accounts for the reductive voltage hysteresis during discharge ($I_k > 0, \operatorname{sgn}(I_k) = +1 \implies V_{h,k} > 0$), while for charging ($I_k < 0, \operatorname{sgn}(I_k) = -1 \implies V_{h,k} < 0$), $-V_{h,k} > 0$ increases terminal voltage, perfectly reflecting physical electrochemical hysteresis loops.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{State-of-Charge Estimation Methodologies}

\subsection{Extended Kalman Filter (EKF)}
The EKF linearizes the nonlinear measurement equation at each step around prior estimate $\hat{\mathbf{x}}_k^-$ via the Jacobian vector $\mathbf{H}_k$~\cite{Plett2004part3}:
\begin{align}\label{eqn:ekf_jacobian}
\mathbf{H}_k^{\text{NMC}} &= \begin{bmatrix} V_{\text{ocv}}'(\widehat{\text{SOC}}_k^-) & -1 & -1 \end{bmatrix}, \nonumber\\
\mathbf{H}_k^{\text{LFP}} &= \begin{bmatrix} V_{\text{ocv}}'(\widehat{\text{SOC}}_k^-) & -1 & -1 & -1 \end{bmatrix},
\end{align}
where $\partial V_t / \partial V_h = -1$ reflects the subtractive nature of discharge hysteresis overpotential. The recursive algorithm executes in two alternating stages:

\emph{1. Time Update (Predict):}
\begin{align}
\hat{\mathbf{x}}_k^- &= \mathbf{A}_{k-1} \hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k-1}^+ + \mathbf{B}_{k-1} I_{k-1} + \mathbf{g}_{k-1} \label{eqn:ekf_pred_state}\\
\mathbf{P}_k^- &= \mathbf{A}_{k-1} \mathbf{P}_{k-1}^+ \mathbf{A}_{k-1}^T + \mathbf{Q} \label{eqn:ekf_pred_cov}
\end{align}
where $\mathbf{g}_{k-1} = \mathbf{0}$ for NMC and satisfies Eq.~\eqref{eqn:hysteresis_discrete} for LFP.

\emph{2. Measurement Update (Correct):}
\begin{align}
e_k &= V_k - \hat{V}_{t,k}^- \label{eqn:ekf_innov}\\
\mathbf{K}_k &= \mathbf{P}_k^- \mathbf{H}_k^T \left( \mathbf{H}_k \mathbf{P}_k^- \mathbf{H}_k^T + R_v \right)^{-1} \label{eqn:ekf_gain}\\
\hat{\mathbf{x}}_k^+ &= \hat{\mathbf{x}}_k^- + \mathbf{K}_k e_k \label{eqn:ekf_upd_state}\\
\mathbf{P}_k^+ &= (\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{K}_k \mathbf{H}_k) \mathbf{P}_k^- (\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{K}_k \mathbf{H}_k)^T + \mathbf{K}_k R_v \mathbf{K}_k^T \label{eqn:ekf_joseph}
\end{align}
with $\hat{V}_{t,k}^- = V_{\text{ocv}}(\widehat{\text{SOC}}_k^-) - \hat{U}_{1,k}^- - \hat{U}_{2,k}^- - I_k R_0$ (augmented with $-\hat{V}_{h,k}^-$ for LFP). Equation~\eqref{eqn:ekf_joseph} implements the stabilized Joseph covariance form, which guarantees symmetry ($\mathbf{P}_k^+ = (\mathbf{P}_k^+)^T$) and strict positive-definiteness ($\mathbf{P}_k^+ \succ 0$) under finite-precision floating-point arithmetic.

\subsection{Adaptive Extended Kalman Filter (AEKF)}
Fixed noise covariances ($\mathbf{Q}, R_v$) degrade filtering performance when measurement conditions vary. The AEKF integrates the Sage-Husa online adaptive noise estimator~\cite{Han2019aekf}:
\begin{align}
\hat{r}_k &= (1 - d_k)\hat{r}_{k-1} + d_k \left( V_k - \hat{V}_k^- \right) \label{eqn:sh_mean}\\
\hat{R}_{v,k} &= (1 - d_k)\hat{R}_{v,k-1} + d_k \left( e_k^2 - \mathbf{H}_k \mathbf{P}_k^- \mathbf{H}_k^T \right) \label{eqn:sh_meas_cov}
\end{align}
where $d_k = (1 - \lambda_{\text{SH}}) / (1 - \lambda_{\text{SH}}^{k})$ is an exponential weighting factor with forgetting factor $\lambda_{\text{SH}} = 0.985$. To prevent filter divergence arising from the structural unidentifiability of simultaneously estimating process noise $\mathbf{Q}_k$ and measurement noise $R_k$ from a single scalar innovation sequence (classic Sage-Husa theorem), $\mathbf{Q}$ is fixed to its calibrated baseline while $\hat{R}_{v,k}$ is updated with a semi-definite projection constraint $\hat{R}_{v,k} = \max(R_{v,\min}, \hat{R}_{v,k})$ to guarantee positive-definiteness.

\subsection{Dual Extended Kalman Filter (DEKF)}
To decouple fast state estimation from slow parameter degradation, the DEKF employs two interacting Kalman filters running in parallel~\cite{Plett2004part3,Charkhgard2010dekf}: (1)~\emph{State Filter}: Estimates $\mathbf{x}_k = [\text{SOC}_k, U_{1,k}, U_{2,k}]^T$ using current parameter estimates $\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}_k = [\hat{R}_{0,k}, 1/\hat{Q}_{\text{act},k}]^T$; (2)~\emph{Parameter Filter}: Models parameter evolution as a random walk $\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1} = \boldsymbol{\theta}_k + \mathbf{w}_{\theta,k}$ with $\mathbf{w}_{\theta,k} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \mathbf{Q}_\theta)$. The parameter sensitivity matrix $\mathbf{C}_{\theta,k} = \left. \frac{d V_k}{d \boldsymbol{\theta}} \right|_{\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}_k^-}$ is derived via total Fréchet differentiation:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:dekf_param_jacobian}
\mathbf{C}_{\theta,k} = \begin{bmatrix} -I_k & -\frac{\eta \Delta t I_{k-1}}{3600} V_{\text{ocv}}'(\widehat{\text{SOC}}_k^+) \end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}
where $\frac{\partial V_k}{\partial \theta_2} = \frac{\partial V_{\text{ocv}}}{\partial \text{SOC}_k} \frac{\partial \text{SOC}_k}{\partial (1/Q_{\text{act}})} = V_{\text{ocv}}' \left(-\frac{\eta \Delta t I_{k-1}}{3600}\right)$. The parameter filter updates recursively using the stabilized Joseph covariance form:
\begin{align}
\mathbf{K}_{\theta,k} &= \mathbf{P}_{\theta,k}^- \mathbf{C}_{\theta,k}^T \left( \mathbf{C}_{\theta,k} \mathbf{P}_{\theta,k}^- \mathbf{C}_{\theta,k}^T + R_{\theta,v} \right)^{-1} \label{eqn:dekf_param_gain}\\
\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}_k^+ &= \hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}_k^- + \mathbf{K}_{\theta,k} e_k \label{eqn:dekf_param_upd}\\
\mathbf{P}_{\theta,k}^+ &= (\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{K}_{\theta,k} \mathbf{C}_{\theta,k}) \mathbf{P}_{\theta,k}^- (\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{K}_{\theta,k} \mathbf{C}_{\theta,k})^T + \mathbf{K}_{\theta,k} R_{\theta,v} \mathbf{K}_{\theta,k}^T \label{eqn:dekf_param_cov}
\end{align}

\subsection{Sliding Mode Observer (SMO)}
The SMO provides robust convergence against bounded modeling disturbances by driving the voltage error to a sliding manifold $S_k = V_{\text{meas},k} - \hat{V}_k = 0$~\cite{Kim2011,Chen2016smo}:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:smo_state_eq}
\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k+1} = \mathbf{A} \hat{\mathbf{x}}_k + \mathbf{B} I_k + \mathbf{L} \cdot \text{sat}\left( \frac{S_k}{\mu} \right),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{L} = [l_1, l_2, l_3]^T$ denotes the observer feedback gain vector, $\mu = 0.02\text{ V}$ is boundary layer thickness, and continuous saturation is defined as $\text{sat}(S/\mu) = \max(-1, \min(1, S/\mu))$. As proven in Appendix~\ref{app:lyapunov}, enforcing the effective injection gain condition $\gamma(\hat{z}) \equiv \mathbf{H} \mathbf{L} = l_1 V_{\text{ocv}}'(\hat{z}) - l_2 - l_3 \ge \Phi + \eta_s > 0$ with disturbance bound $\Phi$ and margin $\eta_s > 0$ satisfies the Lyapunov reachability criterion $S \dot{S} < 0$, guaranteeing finite-time reaching to the boundary layer $|S| \le \mu$ and Uniform Ultimate Boundedness (UUB) of the state estimation error.

\subsection{Bidirectional LSTM with Self-Attention (BiLSTM-Attn)}
The deep data-driven architecture processes a temporal sliding window $\mathbf{X}_k = [I_{k-W:k}, V_{k-W:k}, T_{k-W:k}] \in \mathbb{R}^{W \times 3}$ with window length $W = 30\text{ s}$~\cite{Song2019bilstm,Vaswani2017attention}. Forward ($\overrightarrow{\mathbf{h}}_t$) and backward ($\overleftarrow{\mathbf{h}}_t$) hidden states are concatenated: $\mathbf{h}_t = [\overrightarrow{\mathbf{h}}_t \,\|\, \overleftarrow{\mathbf{h}}_t] \in \mathbb{R}^{2 D}$. The temporal self-attention mechanism computes alignment scores:
\begin{align}
u_t &= \mathbf{v}^T \tanh(\mathbf{W}_a \mathbf{h}_t + \mathbf{b}_a), \quad \alpha_t = \frac{\exp(u_t)}{\sum_{j=1}^W \exp(u_j)} \label{eqn:attn_weight}\\
\mathbf{c}_k &= \sum_{t=1}^W \alpha_t \mathbf{h}_t \label{eqn:attn_context}
\end{align}
where $\mathbf{c}_k$ represents the context vector fed into fully connected layers to predict $\widehat{\text{SOC}}_k$.

\subsection{Physics-Informed Neural Network (PINN-SOC)}
Unlike conventional black-box networks that treat SOC estimation as an unconstrained regression task, the Physics-Informed Neural Network (PINN-SOC) embeds first-principles electrochemical conservation laws directly into the backpropagation graph~\cite{Raissi2019pinn,Brancato2026,Li2023nature}.

\emph{1. Continuous State-Space and Automatic Differentiation}: From Fickian lithium-ion diffusion and macroscopic charge conservation, battery charge depletion satisfies the continuous governing equation:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:pinn_ode}
\frac{d\,\text{SOC}(t)}{dt} = -\frac{\eta(T, I)\, I(t)}{3600\,Q_{\text{act}}(T, \text{SOH})},
\end{equation}
where $\eta \in (0, 1]$ represents Faradaic coulombic efficiency capturing side reactions, and $Q_{\text{act}}$ denotes usable capacity modulated by Arrhenius thermal kinetics and SEI growth. A deep feedforward surrogate parameterized by $\boldsymbol{\theta}$ predicts $[\widehat{\text{SOC}}(t), \hat{V}_{\text{pol}}(t)]^T = \mathcal{NN}(I(t), V_t(t), T(t); \boldsymbol{\theta})$, outputting both the latent SOC and the unmeasured internal polarization overpotential. Crucially, the temporal derivative $\partial \widehat{\text{SOC}}/\partial t$ is evaluated analytically via PyTorch Automatic Differentiation (Autograd) across the computational graph, eliminating finite-difference approximation errors. The continuous Faradaic residual is:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:res_faraday}
\mathcal{R}_{\text{Faraday}}(t) = \frac{\partial \widehat{\text{SOC}}(t)}{\partial t} + \frac{\eta(T, I)\, I(t)}{3600\,Q_{\text{act}}(T, \text{SOH})}.
\end{equation}

\emph{2. Electrochemical KVL Consistency and Thermodynamic Bounds}: Operational battery pack sensors cannot non-invasively measure internal overpotentials ($U_1, U_2$). PINN-SOC resolves this observability deficit by enforcing Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL) as an electrochemical consistency manifold:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:res_kvl}
\mathcal{R}_{\text{KVL}}(t) = V_{\text{meas}}(t) - \left[ V_{\text{ocv}}(\widehat{\text{SOC}}(t)) - I(t) R_0(T, \text{SOH}) - \hat{V}_{\text{pol}}(t) \right].
\end{equation}
Thermodynamic stability is ensured by requiring strict monotonicity of the open-circuit voltage curve ($d V_{\text{ocv}}/d\,\text{SOC} > 0$), while an output Sigmoid activation intrinsically guarantees physical boundary invariance $\widehat{\text{SOC}} \in (0, 1)$.

\emph{3. Composite Multi-Objective Loss Formulation}: The composite training loss balances supervised empirical fidelity over labeled points $\mathcal{D}_{\text{data}} = \{(I_k, V_k, T_k, \text{SOC}_k^{\text{ref}})\}_{k=1}^{N_d}$ with physical governing residuals evaluated over dense, unmeasured collocation points $\mathcal{D}_{\text{colloc}} = \{(I_j, V_j, T_j)\}_{j=1}^{N_c}$:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:pinn_loss}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = \mathcal{L}_{\text{data}} + \lambda_{\text{Faraday}} \mathcal{L}_{\text{Faraday}} + \lambda_{\text{KVL}} \mathcal{L}_{\text{KVL}},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{L}_{\text{data}} = \frac{1}{N_d} \sum_{k=1}^{N_d} (\widehat{\text{SOC}}_k - \text{SOC}_k^{\text{ref}})^2$ denotes supervised loss, $\mathcal{L}_{\text{Faraday}} = \frac{1}{N_c} \sum_{j=1}^{N_c} \|\mathcal{R}_{\text{Faraday}}(t_j)\|^2$ represents Faradaic consistency, and $\mathcal{L}_{\text{KVL}} = \frac{1}{N_c} \sum_{j=1}^{N_c} \|\mathcal{R}_{\text{KVL}}(t_j)\|^2$ represents KVL tracking ($\lambda_{\text{Faraday}} = 0.40$, $\lambda_{\text{KVL}} = 0.30$).

\subsection{Proposed Hybrid Architecture: PIDKF}
While model-based observers offer closed-loop stability with guaranteed Lyapunov convergence and online parameter identification, their state models rely on low-order linear RC approximations that omit complex nonlinear diffusion and kinetic overpotentials. Conversely, standalone neural networks require extensive training data and lack error covariance propagation. To resolve this trade-off, we formulate the hybrid \textbf{Physics-Informed Dual Kalman Filter (PIDKF)}.

In PIDKF, a dual extended Kalman filter executes online co-estimation of the bulk electrical state $\hat{\mathbf{x}}_k$ and degradation parameters $\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}_k = [R_{0,k}, 1/Q_{\text{act},k}]^T$. Concurrently, a compact physics-informed neural compensator (input vector $\mathbf{u}_k = [I_k, T_k, \widehat{\text{SOC}}_k]^T$, two hidden layers of 16 and 8 units with LeakyReLU activations) trained on dynamic stress cycles predicts unmodeled polarization voltage residual $\delta \hat{V}_{\text{pol},k} = \mathcal{NN}(\mathbf{u}_k; \boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{net}})$. The target residual is defined by the signed voltage discrepancy $\delta V_{\text{pol},k}^{\text{target}} \equiv V_{\text{meas},k} - \hat{V}_{t,\text{ECM},k}^-$. The corrected terminal prediction $\hat{V}_{t,\text{PIDKF},k}^- = \hat{V}_{t,\text{ECM},k}^- + \delta \hat{V}_{\text{pol},k}$ yields the innovation residual $e_{k,\text{PIDKF}} = V_{\text{meas},k} - \hat{V}_{t,\text{PIDKF},k}^- = \delta V_{\text{pol},k}^{\text{target}} - \delta \hat{V}_{\text{pol},k}$.

The neural compensator is optimized via the dedicated composite loss function:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:loss_pidkf}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{PIDKF}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{net}}) = \mathcal{L}_{\text{res}} + \lambda_{\text{phys}} \mathcal{L}_{\text{phys}} + \lambda_{\text{reg}} \|\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{net}}\|_2^2,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{L}_{\text{res}} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=1}^N (\delta V_{\text{pol},k}^{\text{target}} - \delta \hat{V}_{\text{pol},k})^2$, and the physics constraint enforces Butler-Volmer overpotential bounds $V_{\text{pol}}^{\max}(I_k) = \kappa_0 + \kappa_1 |I_k|$ and thermodynamic dissipative polarity ($I_k \delta \hat{V}_{\text{pol},k} \le 0$): $\mathcal{L}_{\text{phys}} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=1}^N [ \max(0, |\delta \hat{V}_{\text{pol},k}| - V_{\text{pol}}^{\max})^2 + \gamma_I \max(0, I_k \delta \hat{V}_{\text{pol},k})^2 ]$. This regularizer guarantees that the compensator respects electrochemical overpotential limits and dissipative polarity, preventing the network from acting as an unphysical energy source or fitting sensor noise.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Experimental Benchmarking Platform and Methodology}

\subsection{Benchmark Platform and Empirical Calibration Data}
The benchmark framework is established upon a high-fidelity electro-thermal-aging simulation platform calibrated against open-access empirical cycling datasets from the Center for Advanced Life Cycle Engineering (CALCE, University of Maryland)~\cite{Pecht2011calce} and Oxford University~\cite{Birkl2017diagnostics}. Calibration utilized empirical continuous aging datasets of commercial cylindrical Samsung INR18650-20R ($2.00\text{ A}\cdot\text{h}$ nominal NMC, CALCE cell \texttt{CS2\_35}/\allowbreak\texttt{INR20R}) and CALCE A123 26650 ($2.30\text{ A}\cdot\text{h}$ LFP, CALCE cell \texttt{A123\_Pack}) cycled across ambient temperatures ($0^\circ\text{C}, 25^\circ\text{C}, 45^\circ\text{C}$) and SOH levels ($100\%$ down to $80\%$). 

Offline calibration ($R_0, R_i, C_i, V_{\text{ocv}}(z)$) was performed strictly on reference characterization cycles (C/20 CC-CV titration and 50 early-life training cycles under UDDS/DST at $25^\circ\text{C}$). Algorithms were then benchmarked on independent hold-out test datasets: separate driving cycles (US06, FUDS), unseen aging checkpoints (cycles 100, 200, 400, 600 corresponding to $95\%, 90\%, 85\%, 80\%$ SOH), and unseen ambient temperatures ($0^\circ\text{C}, 45^\circ\text{C}$). SOC ground truth was determined via Coulomb counting at 10 Hz from high-precision battery cycler channels (Arbin BT-2000 multi-channel system: $\pm 0.02\%$ voltage error, $\pm 0.05\%$ current error), re-zeroed at C/20 CC-CV cutoffs after 2-hour rest relaxation. Embedded execution profiling is established via analytical instruction-level cycle counting based on ARM CMSIS-DSP (v5.9.0) and CMSIS-NN computational kernels compiled via ARM GCC 12.3 (-O3) on Cortex-M4 (168 MHz) and Cortex-M7 (480 MHz) architectures, verified against hardware DWT cycle counters. Host inference latency was measured on an Intel Core i7-12700H @ 2.70 GHz (batch size = 1, $10^5$ iterations post-warm-up).

\subsection{Standardized Automotive Dynamic Stress Cycles}
Four standardized driving cycles replicate realistic vehicular conditions: (1)~\emph{UDDS} captures urban stop-and-go driving with regenerative braking transients ($1369\text{ s}$); (2)~\emph{US06} represents aggressive highway driving characterized by high acceleration and up to $4\text{C}$ discharge pulses ($600\text{ s}$); (3)~\emph{DST} implements standard USABC dynamic step-wise power profiles; and (4)~\emph{FUDS} evaluates deep multi-rate urban dynamic transitions. Urban (UDDS) and aggressive dynamic (FUDS) schedules serve as primary reported test schedules, while US06 and DST verify generalization.

\subsection{Quantitative Performance Indices}
Algorithm performance is rigorously evaluated using four statistical metrics:
\begin{align}
\text{RMSE} &= \sqrt{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=1}^N \left(\text{SOC}_k^{\text{true}} - \widehat{\text{SOC}}_k\right)^2} \times 100\% \label{eqn:rmse}\\
\text{MAE} &= \frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=1}^N \left|\text{SOC}_k^{\text{true}} - \widehat{\text{SOC}}_k\right| \times 100\% \label{eqn:mae}\\
\text{MaxE} &= \max_{1 \le k \le N} \left|\text{SOC}_k^{\text{true}} - \widehat{\text{SOC}}_k\right| \times 100\% \label{eqn:maxe}\\
t_{\text{conv}} &= \min\left\{ t^* \;\middle|\; \left|\text{SOC}_k^{\text{true}} - \widehat{\text{SOC}}_k\right| \le 2.0\%, \;\forall k \ge t^* \right\} \label{eqn:tconv}
\end{align}
To evaluate degradation robustness, we define the \textbf{Aging Sensitivity Factor (ASF)}:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:asf}
\text{ASF} = \frac{\text{RMSE}(\text{SOH}=80\%) - \text{RMSE}(\text{SOH}=100\%)}{\text{RMSE}(\text{SOH}=100\%)} \times 100\%.
\end{equation}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Results and In-Depth Discussion Across 10 Operational Phases}

\subsection{Phase 1: Dynamic Drive Cycle Tracking at Nominal Condition}
Table~\ref{tab:1_udds} and Fig.~\ref{fig:udds} present the comparative performance of the benchmarked algorithms across the full UDDS profile on a fresh cell ($\text{SOH} = 100\%$) at $25^\circ\text{C}$.

Under nominal conditions with known parameters, the classical EKF achieves the lowest overall tracking error ($\text{RMSE} = 0.065\%$, $\text{MAE} = 0.050\%$, $\text{MaxE} = 1.067\%$). The Dual EKF achieves an RMSE of $0.191\%$ and MAE of $0.159\%$, while delivering the lowest maximum peak error among all evaluated algorithms ($\text{MaxE} = 0.673\%$), demonstrating that concurrent online parameter adjustment prevents localized divergence during steep dynamic transitions. The Adaptive EKF yields an RMSE of $0.759\%$ ($\text{MAE} = 0.610\%$, $\text{MaxE} = 1.490\%$), where the Sage-Husa covariance estimator experiences mild sensitivity to rapid current transitions. 

The Sliding Mode Observer maintains robust tracking ($\text{RMSE} = 0.941\%$, $\text{MAE} = 0.755\%$, $\text{MaxE} = 3.423\%$), executing in $76.46\;\mu\text{s}$ per step with high-frequency chattering suppressed by the boundary layer $\mu$. Meanwhile, data-driven BiLSTM-Attn and PINN-SOC report RMSE values of $2.813\%$ and $2.781\%$ ($\text{MaxE} = 6.915\%$ and $7.317\%$), respectively. Notably, PINN-SOC demonstrates an ultra-fast host inference latency of $1.22\;\mu\text{s}$ per step on desktop x86\_64, whereas BiLSTM-Attn requires $2643.90\;\mu\text{s}$ per step due to recursive cell unrolling and self-attention operations. Embedded target latencies for microcontroller execution are evaluated separately in Section~\ref{sec:hw_profiling}.

