\subsection[Phase 2: Progressive Aging Degradation]{Phase 2: Progressive Aging Degradation ($100\% \rightarrow 80\%$ SOH)}
Figure~\ref{fig:aging} and Table~\ref{tab:2_aging} present the pivotal finding of this study: the catastrophic divergence of fixed-parameter observers under progressive battery aging.

When cell capacity degrades from $100\%$ to $80\%$ and internal resistance increases, the standard EKF experiences a pronounced \textbf{45-fold accuracy collapse}, with RMSE escalating from $0.065\%$ to $2.973\%$ ($\text{ASF} = +4473.8\%$). The AEKF exhibits a comparable performance deterioration, escalating from $0.759\%$ to $5.406\%$ ($\text{ASF} = +612.3\%$), demonstrating that adapting noise covariances ($\mathbf{Q}, R$) cannot resolve deterministic physical model mismatch. The fundamental mechanism driving this failure is that uncompensated filters attribute aging-induced ohmic overpotentials ($I \cdot \Delta R_0$) to changes in open-circuit voltage, generating a persistent, non-decaying SOC offset. The Sliding Mode Observer error likewise climbs from $0.941\%$ to $5.428\%$ ($\text{ASF} = +476.8\%$).

In stark contrast, the Dual EKF dynamically identifies $R_0$ and $Q_{\text{act}}$ online. Consequently, its RMSE increases only modestly from $0.191\%$ to $0.585\%$ at $80\%$ SOH ($\text{ASF} = +206.3\%$), achieving sub-$0.6\%$ accuracy throughout the entire battery lifespan. PINN-SOC also exhibits consistent degradation tracking ($\text{RMSE} = 4.911\%$, $\text{ASF} = +76.6\%$), while BiLSTM-Attn exhibits an ASF of $+82.3\%$ ($\text{RMSE} = 5.128\%$ at $80\%$ SOH).

