\caption{Evolution of SOC estimation error as battery SOH degrades from $100\%$ down to $80\%$, illustrating the 45-fold accuracy degradation of standard EKF versus the resilience of DEKF\label{fig:aging}}
\end{figure}

\subsection[Phase 3: Thermal Robustness Across Wide Environmental Regimes (0 to 45 deg C)]{Phase 3: Thermal Robustness Across Wide Environmental Regimes ($0^\circ\text{C}$ to $45^\circ\text{C}$)}
Figure~\ref{fig:thermal} and Table~\ref{tab:3_thermal} illustrate algorithmic tracking performance across $0^\circ\text{C}$, $25^\circ\text{C}$, and $45^\circ\text{C}$.

At $0^\circ\text{C}$, electrolyte conductivity drops sharply and charge-transfer resistance $R_{\text{ct}}$ increases markedly via Arrhenius kinetics (Eqs.~\eqref{eqn:arrh_r0}--\eqref{eqn:arrh_ri}). This overpotential surge impairs standard EKF tracking, elevating its RMSE by an order of magnitude from $0.065\%$ to $0.669\%$. AEKF and DEKF reach RMSE values of $3.178\%$ and $2.254\%$ under the severe low-temperature polarization transient. Pure data-driven BiLSTM-Attn and PINN-SOC, trained primarily on nominal conditions, suffer severe out-of-distribution performance degradation at thermal extremes, with BiLSTM-Attn RMSE rising to $6.457\%$ at $0^\circ\text{C}$ and $9.462\%$ at $45^\circ\text{C}$, and PINN-SOC reaching $6.307\%$ at $0^\circ\text{C}$ and $9.644\%$ at $45^\circ\text{C}$.

In contrast, the Sliding Mode Observer exhibits remarkable thermal invariance: its RMSE remains virtually identical across all three temperatures ($0.929\%$ at $0^\circ\text{C}$, $0.941\%$ at $25^\circ\text{C}$, and $0.932\%$ at $45^\circ\text{C}$), demonstrating that its discontinuous switching control surface provides inherent robustness against temperature-induced parameter variations.

