\subsection[Phase 4: Convergence Under Large Initial State Uncertainty]{Phase 4: Convergence Under Large Initial State Uncertainty ($\Delta\text{SOC}_0 = -40\%$)}
In real-world BMS operation, initialization errors arise from prolonged parking, self-discharge, or battery replacement. Figure~\ref{fig:conv} and Table~\ref{tab:4_conv} depict the convergence trajectories when an initial error of $\Delta\text{SOC}_0 = -40\%$ ($\widehat{\text{SOC}}_0 = 0.50$ versus $\text{SOC}_0^{\text{true}} = 0.90$) is imposed.

The Adaptive EKF and standard EKF achieve rapid, smooth convergence into the $\le 2.0\%$ target bound within $6.0\text{ s}$ and $7.0\text{ s}$ respectively, maintaining bounded overall RMSE ($0.683\%$ and $0.686\%$) and MaxE ($4.43\%$). The Sliding Mode Observer reaches the sliding manifold within $9.0\text{ s}$ with an RMSE of $1.948\%$. BiLSTM-Attn requires $30.0\text{ s}$ (the duration of its temporal memory window) to purge erroneous history ($\text{RMSE} = 5.625\%$). For DEKF, the severe $-40\%$ initial SOC shock initially perturbs the parameter identification filter, taking $725.0\text{ s}$ to fully settle into the strict $\le 2.0\%$ threshold, although state tracking recovers quickly and remains bounded ($\text{MaxE} = 7.67\%$). In contrast, PINN-SOC operates as a feedforward static mapping without a recursive state loop or covariance settling ($t_{\text{conv}} = \text{N/A}$, $\text{RMSE} = 2.229\%$, $\text{MaxE} = 6.84\%$), rendering recursive dynamic convergence metrics not applicable.

