\subsection[Phase 6: Multi-Chemistry Generalization: LiFePO4 (LFP) Flat Plateau]{Phase 6: Multi-Chemistry Generalization: $\text{LiFePO}_4$ (LFP) Flat Plateau}
Figure~\ref{fig:lfp} and Table~\ref{tab:5_lfp} report estimation performance on LFP chemistry. In the plateau region ($20\%\text{--}80\%$ SOC), the slope $d V_{\text{ocv}} / d\,\text{SOC}$ falls near zero ($\approx 0.08\text{ V}$), causing the observability Gramian condition number $\kappa(\mathcal{O})$ to surge. 

Consequently, the observation vector $\mathbf{H}_k \approx [0, -1, -1, -1]$ loses sensitivity to SOC variations. Standard EKF suffers a $+1648.4\%$ performance degradation, escalating from $0.065\%$ on NMC to $1.136\%$ on LFP ($\text{MAE} = 0.939\%$, $\text{MaxE} = 2.410\%$). AEKF similarly degrades by $+281.1\%$ to $2.893\%$ RMSE. The Sliding Mode Observer experiences severe chattering along the flat plateau, escalating by $+919.5\%$ to $9.593\%$ RMSE ($\text{MaxE} = 20.935\%$). 

In contrast, DEKF achieves the lowest error among all observers on LFP ($\text{RMSE} = 0.870\%$, $\text{MAE} = 0.649\%$, $\text{MaxE} = 2.018\%$). By continuously adapting equivalent parameters online, DEKF compensates for the loss of OCV sensitivity, proving that advanced electrical observers can track LFP cells without requiring auxiliary mechanical strain or expansion force sensors~\cite{Tian2025}.

