\subsection{Phase 7: Sensor Corruption and Environmental Stress Testing}
Table~\ref{tab:6_sensor} and Fig.~\ref{fig:sensor} document observer resilience under a $+0.25\text{ A}$ current sensor Hall bias drift and $25\text{ mV}$ high-frequency inverter EMI noise ripple.

Open-loop Coulomb counting diverges linearly, accumulating an end-of-cycle drift of $12.399\%$ ($\text{RMSE} = 7.178\%$, $\text{MaxE} = 12.409\%$). The closed-loop Kalman filters effectively arrest this divergence: standard EKF limits RMSE to $1.817\%$ with an end-of-cycle drift of $1.821\%$ ($\text{MaxE} = 5.013\%$). AEKF and DEKF deliver similar robustness, bounding RMSE to $1.859\%$ and $2.121\%$, with end drifts of $2.026\%$ and $1.927\%$, respectively. The Sliding Mode Observer exhibits the lowest terminal end-of-cycle drift ($1.024\%$) due to the strong disturbance-rejection properties of the sliding surface, although switching dynamics yield an overall RMSE of $5.035\%$ and MaxE of $14.458\%$.

