\subsection{Phase 8: Model-Based Electrochemical Impedance Spectroscopy (EIS) Analysis Across Aging}
Figure~\ref{fig:eis} shows the EIS Nyquist spectra ($100\text{ kHz}$--$1\text{ mHz}$) via Randles transfer function $Z(j\omega) = R_0 + (R_{\text{ct}} + Z_w) / [1 + (j\omega C_{\text{dl}})^\alpha (R_{\text{ct}} + Z_w)]$ with Warburg impedance $Z_w(j\omega) = \sigma_w \omega^{-1/2}(1 - j)$ across aging. Bulk ohmic resistance $R_0$ increases from $35.0\text{ m}\Omega$ to $52.5\text{ m}\Omega$ (+50\%), while charge-transfer arc $R_{\text{ct}}$ widens by $>150\%$ ($15.0$ to $37.5\text{ m}\Omega$). This physical impedance expansion directly explains time-domain observer divergence: uncompensated EKF suffers growing overpotential error $\Delta V = I(R_{\text{ct,aged}} - R_{\text{ct,fresh}})$, driving the 45-fold estimation drift.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.80\linewidth]{../results/fig8_eis_nyquist_aging.png}
\caption{Model-based EIS Nyquist spectra across progressive aging stages ($100\%$ down to $80\%$ SOH)\label{fig:eis}}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Phase 9: Module and Pack-Level Scaling (8S Series Pack)}
Table~\ref{tab:7_pack} and Fig.~\ref{fig:pack} evaluate scaling to an 8S series pack with cell manufacturing inconsistency ($\sigma_Q = 3.0\%, \sigma_R = 10.0\%$)~\cite{Yang2024degradation,Zheng2018inconsistency}. While a lumped pack observer tracks mean pack SOC accurately ($\text{RMSE} = 0.128\%, \text{MaxE} = 0.443\%$), it incurs a critical \textbf{1.744\% RMSE and 2.302\% peak error} on the weakest cell, risking over-discharge ($<2.5\text{ V}$) or lithium plating. In contrast, distributed DEKF bounds weakest-cell RMSE to $0.337\%$ and MaxE to $0.963\%$, guaranteeing string safety.

