\subsection{Phase 10: Embedded Hardware Execution on ARM Cortex-M and PIDKF Superiority}\label{sec:hw_profiling}
To resolve the gap between algorithmic complexity and industrial viability, computational complexity was profiled on ARM Cortex-M4 (168 MHz) and Cortex-M7 (480 MHz) microcontrollers using CMSIS-DSP. Results are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:8_embedded} and Fig.~\ref{fig:embedded}.

The Sliding Mode Observer demonstrates ultra-light computational overhead, requiring only 180 FLOPs, 140 Bytes of RAM, and executing in $1.67\;\mu\text{s}$ on Cortex-M4 ($0.58\;\mu\text{s}$ on M7). DEKF requires 980 FLOPs, 620 Bytes of SRAM, and completes in $8.63\;\mu\text{s}$ on Cortex-M4—well within the standard $100\text{ ms}$ BMS execution loop. In contrast, deep learning BiLSTM-Attn demands $245,000$ FLOPs, $18.4\text{ KB}$ SRAM, and takes $2440.48\;\mu\text{s}$ per step, rendering it impractical for resource-constrained automotive MCUs.

Furthermore, Table~\ref{tab:9_pidkf} and Fig.~\ref{fig:pidkf} document the ablation analysis of hybrid \textbf{PIDKF} on degraded cells ($85\%$ SOH). On $\text{LiFePO}_4$ where flat plateaus degrade observability, dynamic hysteresis tracking slashes RMSE from $0.302\%$ to $0.149\%$ ($0.335\%$ MaxE). Neural compensation alone without hysteresis yields $0.246\%$ RMSE, proving neural networks cannot replace thermodynamic hysteresis states. Conversely, on NMC where hysteresis is minor, the physics-informed compensator suppresses kinetic overpotentials, cutting RMSE from $0.452\%$ to $0.414\%$ and peak error by $46.6\%$ ($1.400\%$ to $0.748\%$). Unconstrained black-box MLP degrades accuracy ($0.438\%$ on NMC, $0.285\%$ on LFP) from overfitting noise, confirming regularizer necessity. Full PIDKF attains $0.414\%$ on NMC and $0.149\%$ on LFP ($0.329\%$ MaxE) with 1,540 FLOPs and $14.20\;\mu\text{s}$ on Cortex-M4.

