%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Industrial BMS Implementation Guidelines}
Practical engineering selection rules are established:
(1)~\emph{Premium EVs (NMC/LFP)}: Deploy \textbf{PIDKF}. Suppressing polarization residuals and tracking hysteresis achieves $0.414\%$ RMSE on aged NMC and $0.149\%$ on aged LFP ($85\%$ SOH); standard EKF is discouraged due to 45-fold accuracy collapse.
(2)~\emph{Cost-Sensitive Storage (LFP)}: Employ \textbf{DEKF} with dynamic hysteresis, bounding error to $0.870\%$ and eliminating expensive strain sensors.
(3)~\emph{High-Noise Environments}: Deploy \textbf{SMO} with boundary layer $\mu = 0.02\text{ V}$ for maximum immunity against sensor drift and EMI, limiting drift to $1.024\%$.
(4)~\emph{Cloud-Edge Digital Twins}: Implement a split architecture: embedded MCUs run real-time DEKF ($8.63\;\mu\text{s}$) while cloud servers execute PINN operators for fleet-wide parameter identification and OTA updates.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Conclusions}
This study established a comprehensive benchmark of 6 SOC estimation methodologies across 10 operational phases: 
(1)~\emph{Aging Degradation}: EKF/AEKF suffer a 45-fold error surge ($\text{ASF} > +4400\%$) as cell SOH fades to $80\%$ from uncompensated resistance growth; DEKF bounds aged RMSE to $0.585\%$ ($\text{ASF} = +206.3\%$). 
(2)~\emph{EIS Correlation}: Frequency-domain impedance confirmed that charge-transfer resistance $R_{\text{ct}}$ expands by $>150\%$, directly driving time-domain observer divergence. 
(3)~\emph{LFP Plateau Tracking}: In the zero-slope plateau of $\text{LiFePO}_4$, DEKF maintains $0.870\%$ RMSE via parameter tracking, proving purely electrical observers track LFP cells without mechanical strain sensors. 
(4)~\emph{Pack Inconsistency}: Lumped observers incur $1.744\%$ RMSE and $2.302\%$ peak error on the weakest cell in an 8S string, whereas distributed DEKF confines weakest-cell RMSE to $0.337\%$ and MaxE to $0.963\%$. 
(5)~\emph{Pareto Optimality of PIDKF}: The proposed PIDKF achieves $0.414\%$ RMSE on degraded NMC and $0.149\%$ on degraded LFP ($85\%$ SOH) with $14.20\;\mu\text{s}$ execution time on ARM Cortex-M4, establishing Pareto optimality for industrial BMS deployment.

